The most common you'll hear about are hydrothermal vents.

1182
01:09:02,023 --> 01:09:08,947
These are areas of the crust, typically around spreading zones and where the crust is thinnest.

1183
01:09:10,067 --> 01:09:16,631
Where water can seep down through the rock and get close to magma and get super heated.

1184
01:09:17,151 --> 01:09:22,814
And while it's being super heated, it will start to absorb minerals and metals from the

1185
01:09:22,866 --> 01:09:29,028
rock and just become infused with zinc and iron and copper and lead and cobalt and all

1186
01:09:29,068 --> 01:09:31,009
sorts of good yummy metals.

1187
01:09:31,629 --> 01:09:34,010
Then it's now hot water and hot water rises.

1188
01:09:34,290 --> 01:09:35,110
So a geyser.

1189
01:09:35,430 --> 01:09:37,551
Pumps its way out through the cracks in the rock.

1190
01:09:38,131 --> 01:09:45,394
And as it hits the cold water, those metals will precipitate out of the water and be deposited

1191
01:09:45,566 --> 01:09:50,968
and eventually will form structures around the water flow and form chimneys.

1192
01:09:51,368 --> 01:09:58,631
So it's sort of forming like a volcano where as the lava comes out, it's cooling and creating

1193
01:09:58,672 --> 01:09:59,352
a volcano.

1194
01:09:59,832 --> 01:10:05,915
In this case, it's the minerals coming out and creating, like you said, a chimney or

1195
01:10:06,135 --> 01:10:09,676
a mound built of those yummy things.

1196
01:10:09,725 --> 01:10:09,945
Yes.

1197
01:10:10,845 --> 01:10:15,427
These are typically separated into black smokers, which are pumping out iron sulfide and are

1198
01:10:15,467 --> 01:10:20,389
therefore black in color, and white smokers, which are mostly barium and calcium and silicon

1199
01:10:20,550 --> 01:10:21,610
and are more white in color.

1200
01:10:22,150 --> 01:10:25,231
Black smokers are what mostly I see discussed and shown pictures of.

1201
01:10:25,832 --> 01:10:28,893
These are fairly recently known structures.

1202
01:10:28,933 --> 01:10:31,494
We only discovered these in 1977.

1203
01:10:32,764 --> 01:10:37,726
So we are still learning a ton about these structures and how they function.

1204
01:10:38,186 --> 01:10:43,008
The water being pumped out of these chimneys is extremely hot, 400 degrees Celsius or 752

1205
01:10:43,589 --> 01:10:47,530
degrees Fahrenheit, which I saw listed as hot enough to melt lead.

1206
01:10:48,031 --> 01:10:50,852
Oh, very hot enough to melt sea creatures.

1207
01:10:50,892 --> 01:10:51,152
Yes.

1208
01:10:51,952 --> 01:10:55,074
And there is sea life surrounding these chimneys.

